1.- What tables do you need to add? Decide on table names and their associations to each other and any existing tables/models.
    We need a table to store information about the topics. This table has to have an id, a topic name and a topic description. The table name will be topics and the model name will be Topic.
    We need a join table to associate topics and lesson. This table has to have an id, a reference to a topic and also a reference to a lesson. The table name will be lesson_topics and the model name will be LessonTopic.
2.- What columns are necessary for the associations you decided on?
    We need the id of the topic and the id of the lesson.
3.- What other columns (if any) need to be included on the tables? What other data needs to be stored?
    For the topics table we need a title and a description.
    For the join table we only need the topic_id and the lesson_id
4.- Write out each table's name and column names with data types.
    Table: topics
    Model: Topic
    Columns:
    id - number
    title - string
    description - string

    Table: lesson_topics
    Model: LessonTopic
    Columns:
    id - number
    lesson_id - number
    topic_id - number
5.- Determine the generator command you'll need to create the migration file and run the command to generate the empty migration file. Start with just the topics migration. (Hint: your filename should be create_topics)

amb@ctd1:~/ctdapp$ bin/rails generate migration create_topics
      invoke  active_record
      create    db/migrate/20250820172919_create_topics.rb

6.- Inside the create_table block in the migration file, add the appropriate columns and datatypes.
amb@ctd1:~/ctdapp$ cat db/migrate/20250820172919_create_topics.rb
class CreateTopics < ActiveRecord::Migration[8.0]
  def change
    create_table :topics do |t|
      t.string :title
      t.string :description

      t.timestamps
    end
  end
end

7.- Run the migration with bin/rails db:migrate
amb@ctd1:~/ctdapp$ bin/rails db:migrate
